Condition : 2 - The development shall be carried out in accordance with the reports, specifications and drawings detailed 25:9065:U1:FP:01; 25:9065:U1:FP:04A; 25:9065:U1:FP:05B; 25:9065:U1:FP:06; 
25:9065:U1:FP:07B; 25:9065:U1:FP:08A; 25:9065:U1:FP:09A; 25:9065:U1:FP:11; 25:9065:U1:FP:12; 25:9065:U1:FP:13; BNG Exemption Statement, dated 14.10.2024; OH/9065/U1/PS � Combined Planning & Design & Access Statement, Rev A, prepared by Avis Appleton & Associated, dated 20.11.2025; Daylight and Sunlight Assessment, prepared by EPS, dated November 2025; OH/9065/U1/HS � Heritage Assessment, prepared by Avis Appleton & Associated, dated 20.11.2025; Energy Statement, dated 21.11.2025; OH/9065/U1/FSS � Fire Safety Strategy, prepared by Avis Appleton & Associated, dated 20.11.2025; 24-695 � Phase 2 Contamination Risk Assessment, prepared by Ground and Environmental Investigation Ltd, dated October 2024; Preliminary Ecological Appraisal and Roost Assessment, prepared by Arbtech, dated 01/10/2024 & Metal bike bunker by Secure Cycle Store.  
Reason: To ensure a satisfactory standard of development and to allow the local planning authority to review any potential changes to the scheme.

Condition : 13 - No development shall commence until a Construction and Environmental Management Plan (CEMP) has been submitted to and been approved in writing by the local planning authority. The CEMP shall include measures to mitigate the construction effects as part of a coordinated and collaborative 
approach with surrounding developments, and shall include the following details (although not limited to): 
1. Whilst works should take place outside of the nesting season (March � August inclusive), if works are unavoidable during this period, then a site walkover no more than 48 hours before works commence is required by a CIEMM accredited ecologist, with experience of working in 
London, to check for any nesting birds in the buildings. In the event that a nest is found, an appropriate exclusion zone should be implemented around it until the young have fully fledged (Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 (as amended)). The law protects all wild bird species, their eggs and nests; 
2. Details of measures to be taken to ensure that retained and adjacent habitats will not be impacted negatively by the works (including, but not limited to vehicle movement, lighting, dust, litter, noise etc.); 
3. Whilst construction lighting should be avoided, if lighting is necessary, details of a construction lighting plan outlining how lighting will be prevented from negatively impacting any protected and priority species during construction, in particular commuting bats, or adjacent habitats. This construction lighting plan should follow the best practice industry guidance outlined in the Bat Conservation Trust (BCT) and Institute of Lighting Professionals (ILP) Guidance Note (2023); 
4. Details of measures to prevent harm to or entrapment of wildlife on site, including in excavations and construction and waste materials left on site.
Reason: To ensure construction works and traffic impacts are minimised, to protect amenities of neighbouring occupiers and biodiversity and to mitigate the impact

Condition : 14 - No onsite works, including but not limited to demolition, roof works, replacement of soffits/fasciae, works to ridge tiles, repointing or replacement of tiles/slates, removal of external cladding, or installation/alteration of external lighting, shall commence until a Bat Mitigation Strategy has been 
submitted to and approved in writing by the Local Planning Authority. 
The Strategy shall be prepared by a suitably qualified CIEEM-accredited ecologist with experience of working in London and shall include: 
1. Results of all targeted bat emergence/re-entry surveys necessary to inform mitigation, undertaken during the active bat season (May�September, with the optimal survey period mid-May to August inclusive), and carried out in accordance with the recommendations set out in the Arbtech Preliminary Ecological Appraisal and Roost Assessment (1 October 2024); 
2. A detailed method statement for soft-strip techniques and toolbox talks; 
3. A works schedule tied to the bat activity season and suitable weather conditions; 
4. Precautionary measures, including sensitive hand-removal of features with roosting potential, endoscope checks, and an ecological watching brief where relevant; 
5. Details of contingency and licensing procedures to be followed if bats or a bat roost are encountered, including immediate stop-work, safeguarding measures, and application for any Natural England licence required. 
The development shall thereafter be carried out only in accordance with the approved Bat Mitigation Strategy. 
Reason: To ensure adequate protection of bats, which are European Protected Species, and to avoid harm to individuals or roosts in accordance with relevant wildlife legislation.

Condition : 15 - Notwithstanding the details shown on the approved plans and set out in the supporting information, Landscape and Ecological Enhancement Plan shall be submitted to and approved in writing by the local planning authority prior to the commencement of any above ground works. The Plan shall 
include details of all measures to provide biodiversity gain onsite and offsite, including but not limited to: 
1. Details of planting including species and plans, with priority given to wildlife friendly species; 
2. Orientation, type and location for at least 2x swift brick/box and at least 2x bat bricks/boxes/tiles to be integrated with or attached to the building; 
3. Details of hedgehog holes; 
4. If lighting is proposed, a bat sensitive post-construction lighting plan, to ensure that any lighting will be of a specification that minimises its impacts on bats and onsite and adjacent habitats in accordance with the BCT and ILP 2023 Guidance Note (including having a colour temperature of <2700k, no uplighters and no light spill onto sensitive habitats); 
5. Details of any mitigation and enhancements measures recommended by the bat emergence survey. 
The approved details shall be implemented prior to first occupation of the development and maintained thereafter.
